I think that we should have a consistent default across all of `fetch_*` functions.

But you are definitely right that we shouldn't change the default behavior on `fetch_diabetes_hospital`, where it's effectively `True`, or on all other functions, where it is `False`, without some transition period. Once we decide what the new default should be, we should make sure that those functions where the default will change issue a warning when called without specifying `as_frame`. For example, the warning would be something like:

>You have not specified the value for `as_frame`, which currently defaults to `True`. Starting with version 0.10.0, the default is changing to `False`. Specify the value of `as_frame` to suppress the warning.

I actually think that the more sensible default value is `True` for all `fetch_*` functions. They are really just meant for educational purposes, so returning a DataFrame seems more appropriate. I reviewed our documentation and examples. We are currently using three datasets: `fetch_adult(as_frame=True)`, `fetch_boston(as_frame=True)`, and `fetch_diabetes_hospital()`. So we never use `as_frame=False` (either explicitly or via default). The default `True` would be also closer to [sklearn default for fetch_openml](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.datasets.fetch_openml.html).

